From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:25:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 085/190] segacd: age 1 made streaming impossible; window it at
 3 and cache twelve

Tightening ring entries to age one, two builds ago, cost far more than
the mispairs it stopped. A transfer takes a sector time or two, so the
next consecutive sector -- already decoded, sitting in the buffer --
was always already too old to use: refused, and a fresh seek issued
for data the chip was holding. Walking a FAT is eight consecutive
sectors; it became eight seeks instead of one streaming pass, and a
seek is the only part of a read that can really fail. That is why
tightening did not help and the failures kept scattering across the
same region.

The bound is a window, not a point. The buffer holds about seven
blocks, hardware measured the recycler arriving at five, a transfer
costs one or two -- three is the largest age still certainly valid.

And the cache goes four to twelve, so one seek serves a whole FAT walk
or a whole directory scan. Sectors arriving behind the one asked for
are thirteen milliseconds apart and free; Lunar drains everything
available before yielding, for exactly this reason. Cost is 17 KB of
buffer.

Both cores verify (gpgx wants 4500 frames now rather than 3000 -- the
opt-in self-test finishes later when read-ahead holds the drive
longer).
---
 bios/segacd.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 8466cac..036e3e0 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@ static BOOL cd_ring_find(ULONG lba, UWORD *pt)
              * seven deep -- at age four plus two in flight the slot is
              * being rewritten mid-grab. The BIOS consumes its entries
              * at age zero or one; two is already generous. */
-            if (age <= 1 && age < best) {
+            if (age <= 3 && age < best) {
                 best = age;
                 *pt = cd_ring_pt[i];
                 cd_found_stamp = cd_ring_age[i];
@@ -2097,7 +2097,16 @@ static void cd_park(void)
  * GEMDOS block and the next the head moves on and the stream is lost.
  * Eight seeks is eight seconds when it works, and eight chances to
  * fail when it does not: the window stayed shut. */
-#define CD_CACHE_SECTORS 4
+/* Twelve, not four: one seek should serve a whole structure.
+ *
+ * Walking a FAT is eight consecutive CD sectors and a directory scan
+ * is eight more; at four per fill each of those needed several seeks,
+ * and a seek is the only part of a read that can really fail. Sectors
+ * that arrive on the stream behind the one asked for are thirteen
+ * milliseconds apart and free -- Lunar drains everything available
+ * before it yields, for this reason -- so the only cost of taking
+ * twelve is the buffer. */
+#define CD_CACHE_SECTORS 12
 
 static UBYTE cd_cache[CD_CACHE_SECTORS][CDC_DATA_BYTES];
 
